<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulugh Beg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1449">1449</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulugh Beg was a Timurid ruler and astronomer, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> for decades and died in 1449. He built a great observatory and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in Samarkand and sponsored astronomical tables of great accuracy. His brief reign as supreme ruler ended with his overthrow and death at the instigation of his son.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg.jpg" alt="Ulugh Beg" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulugh_Beg,_Timurid_painting_1425-50.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ulugh Beg was born on 22 March 1394 in Sultaniyeh, during his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and was named Mīrzā Muhammad Tāraghay. He was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> and Gawhar Shad. Ulugh Beg, meaning roughly Great Ruler, was a title and not a personal name; it is the Turkic equivalent of Timur's title Amīr-e Kabīr.</p><p>As a child he travelled through much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> extended his conquests. After Timur's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, and in 1409 the sixteen-year-old Ulugh Beg became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. In 1411 he was named sovereign ruler of the whole of Mavarannahr.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ulugh Beg set out to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> an intellectual centre. Between 1417 and 1420 he built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> on Registan Square and invited astronomers and mathematicians to teach there, among them Qadi Zada al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> and later Jamshid al-Kashi. His best-known pupil in astronomy was Ali Qushchi. He built an observatory in Samarkand between 1424 and 1429, whose largest instrument, the Fakhri sextant, had a radius of about 36 metres. With it he compiled a star catalogue, the Zij-i-Sultani, based on new observations, which is generally considered the best between those of Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a>.</p><p>He measured the length of the sidereal year and the Earth's axial tilt, and wrote trigonometric tables of sines and tangents correct to at least eight decimal places. He also kept up diplomacy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, exchanging envoys with the Yongle emperor from 1416. His military record was weaker. A campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> in 1425 left its ruler Shir Muhammad in power, and in 1427 his army was defeated by Baraq, Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sighnaq">Sighnaq</a>. It was the last campaign he led against a neighbouring power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> in 1447, Ulugh Beg marched against his nephew Ala al-Dawla, who had claimed rule at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, and was defeated there in 1448 by Abul-Qasim Babur Mirza. His eldest son Abdal-Latif Mirza then rebelled against him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, and Ulugh Beg surrendered to him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p>Abdal-Latif allowed him to set out on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> but had him assassinated in 1449. His nephew Abdallah Mirza later restored his reputation and placed his remains at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s feet in the Gur-e-Amir, where Soviet archaeologists found them in 1941. A crater on the Moon, named by Johann Heinrich von Mädler on his 1830 map, and the asteroid 2439 Ulugbek bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulugh Beg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Timur and his successors, among them the astronomer-prince <b>Ulugh Beg</b>, its capital at Samarkand became one of the great centres of Islamic art, architecture and scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ulugh Beg</b>'s Samarkand star catalogue of over a thousand stars was among the most accurate before the telescope, and was published at Oxford in 1665.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Conquered by the Arabs in the eighth century, Transoxiana became a focal point of the Islamic Golden Age under the Samanids, was razed by the Mongols in the thirteenth century and flowered again under Timur, whose Samarkand became a centre of science under his grandson <b>Ulugh Beg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultaniyya">Soltaniyeh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.44,48.8&amp;year=1439" aria-label="Soltaniyeh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5777 850 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ulugh Beg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6227 1120h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6227 1120h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.44,48.8&amp;year=1439">What was here in 1439? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
